VALUES ('64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 'secplus-practice-3', 'CompTIA Security+ (SY0-701) Practice Paper 3', 'comptia-sec-plus', ARRAY['Digital Signatures', 'AAA Framework', 'BEC', 'CSRF', 'Zero-Day', 'DDoS', 'DLP', 'PAM', 'Vendor Risk']::TEXT[], 20, 'standard', true, 3, 30);

-- ── Domain: General Security Concepts (12% — 2 questions) ──

VALUES ('2d350c50-e999-4a4c-b8bf-eb151c5cbe33', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 1, 'A company needs a mechanism so that when an executive digitally signs a contract, the recipient can verify BOTH that the contract has not been altered since signing AND that it genuinely came from that specific executive — such that the executive cannot later plausibly deny having signed it. What cryptographic mechanism provides this, and how does it differ from a plain hash?');
VALUES ('2d350c50-e999-4a4c-b8bf-eb151c5cbe33', '030c98ff-7d23-4774-811f-8079190b1820', 'A digital signature combines hashing (for integrity — a fixed-size fingerprint that changes if the document changes) with asymmetric encryption (for non-repudiation): the signer hashes the document, then encrypts that hash using their OWN PRIVATE key (the reverse of typical asymmetric use, where a public key encrypts and a private key decrypts). Anyone can then decrypt that signature using the signer''s freely available PUBLIC key and compare it to a fresh hash of the document — successful verification proves both that the document is unaltered AND that specifically the private-key holder produced the signature, since only they could have created a signature decryptable with the matching public key, and they cannot plausibly claim someone else signed it. A plain hash alone (covered in Paper 2) proves the document matches a known value, but proves nothing about WHO produced that value — hashing alone gives integrity, not non-repudiation, which is exactly why digital signatures add the asymmetric-encryption layer on top. Symmetric encryption with a SHARED key does not provide non-repudiation, since either party could have produced the encrypted content — non-repudiation specifically requires something only ONE party possesses, which a shared secret is not.');

VALUES ('12be1187-cdcb-4f47-9b4b-3e0cbb4c4bfa', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 2, 'A company''s login system verifies a user''s identity, then determines what that specific user is allowed to do, and separately logs everything the user actually did during their session for later review. What framework describes these three distinct functions?');
VALUES ('12be1187-cdcb-4f47-9b4b-3e0cbb4c4bfa', 'b0a1163e-d12f-4e03-b5cc-964f5e45fe7e', 'The AAA framework names three distinct, sequential functions that are commonly confused precisely because they often happen in the same login flow: Authentication answers "who are you" (verifying identity — a password, MFA, a certificate); Authorization answers "what are you allowed to do" (evaluated AFTER identity is confirmed — e.g., checking role-based permissions); and Accounting (sometimes called auditing) answers "what did you actually do" (logging actions for later review, non-repudiation, and forensic investigation). A user can be successfully AUTHENTICATED (their identity is confirmed) but still be denied a specific action because they are not AUTHORIZED for it — these are genuinely separate checks, not one combined step, which is exactly the nuance this question tests. This is a completely separate framework from the CIA triad (confidentiality/integrity/availability, covered in Paper 1) — despite the coincidental shared "triad of three letters" shape, they describe entirely different concepts (data protection goals vs. access-control functions) and should never be conflated.');

-- ── Domain: Threats, Vulnerabilities & Mitigations (22% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('270e41f7-4ec8-4ca8-a250-b15a7482ef9c', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 3, 'An attacker researches a company''s vendor relationships and sends an email, spoofed to appear from a known supplier''s finance department, requesting an urgent change to the bank account used for an upcoming invoice payment. An employee in accounts payable updates the payment details as requested, and funds are wired to the attacker''s account. What is this specific attack called?');
VALUES ('270e41f7-4ec8-4ca8-a250-b15a7482ef9c', 'c0c6d341-5897-4a85-8eb3-7ccf1ee245f6', 'Business Email Compromise is a specific, high-financial-impact social engineering category distinct from broader phishing/whaling (covered in Paper 1): it specifically targets business processes involving money movement or sensitive data, typically by impersonating a trusted party (a vendor, an executive, a known business contact) via a spoofed or lookalike email address, and relies entirely on social manipulation and process exploitation — NOT malware, exploits, or any technical compromise of systems — making it genuinely difficult to catch with technical controls alone (email filtering, antivirus) and a reason BEC-specific verification procedures (like a mandatory phone callback to a KNOWN, independently-verified number before changing payment details) are a standard, recommended mitigation. A worm is self-replicating malware (a technical, code-based threat) — an entirely different mechanism from a purely social-engineering-based BEC scheme. Rootkits and buffer overflows are also both technical/code-level concepts, unrelated to this social-engineering-only attack pattern.');

VALUES ('84fbe85f-7e3f-473e-b720-3c76672640c0', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 4, 'A vulnerable web application allows an attacker to craft a malicious link that, when clicked by an already-logged-in victim, causes the victim''s browser to unknowingly submit a state-changing request (like transferring funds) using the victim''s own valid session — without the attacker ever obtaining the victim''s session credentials directly. What is this vulnerability called, and how does it differ from XSS?');
VALUES ('84fbe85f-7e3f-473e-b720-3c76672640c0', '0fb6c7c5-8e1d-440a-8ca0-ae4ec959bf4a', 'CSRF exploits the fact that a browser automatically attaches a user''s existing session credentials (like a cookie) to any request sent to that site, regardless of what triggered the request — an attacker crafts a malicious link/form on a DIFFERENT site that, when the victim (who is already logged into the TARGET site) visits it, causes their browser to silently submit a legitimate-looking, authenticated request to the target site, performing an action the victim never intended. This is genuinely different from XSS (covered conceptually via SQL injection in Paper 1''s adjacent web-vulnerability content), which involves injecting and EXECUTING attacker-controlled script code that runs directly within a vulnerable page''s context — CSRF forges a REQUEST using existing trust/session, while XSS injects and runs CODE. Standard CSRF mitigations include anti-CSRF tokens (a unique, unpredictable value required on state-changing requests that an attacker''s cross-site request cannot know) and checking the request''s origin/referrer. SQL injection (covered in Paper 1) manipulates database queries via unsanitized input — an entirely different vulnerability class and mechanism from either CSRF or XSS.');

VALUES ('a04050ac-3786-46c1-a9f5-cb8cc40476a2', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 5, 'A vulnerability in a widely used software library is actively being exploited by attackers in the wild before the vendor has released any patch, and before the vulnerability was even publicly known to exist. What is this type of vulnerability called, and why does routine patch management not protect against it?');
VALUES ('a04050ac-3786-46c1-a9f5-cb8cc40476a2', '4829a24b-5cc0-4c9a-acd7-d16f49042f13', 'A zero-day vulnerability is specifically one being exploited (or at minimum, publicly known) BEFORE a patch exists to fix it — the term reflects that defenders have had "zero days" to prepare a fix since the vulnerability became known (or, in the worst case of active in-the-wild exploitation of a genuinely unknown flaw, even less than that). This is precisely WHY routine patch management (covered in Paper 2) — which fundamentally relies on a vendor-released patch existing to apply — provides no protection during the zero-day window: there is nothing to patch yet. This is exactly why security programs layer OTHER defenses that don''t depend on a specific known vulnerability having a specific known fix: behavioral detection tools like EDR (covered in Paper 1, which can catch anomalous BEHAVIOR even from an unknown exploit), network segmentation limiting blast radius, and least privilege limiting what a successful exploit can actually access. Zero-days affect software (and firmware) broadly, not exclusively hardware, and patches are explicitly NOT released simultaneously with discovery in the zero-day scenario — that absence of an available patch is the entire defining characteristic of the term.');

VALUES ('f76952e0-1bb3-4e07-a6d5-2878a3042661', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 19, 'An attacker sets up a wireless access point in a coffee shop broadcasting the same network name (SSID) as the shop''s legitimate Wi-Fi, with a stronger signal, so nearby devices connect to the attacker''s access point instead of the real one — letting the attacker intercept all traffic from anyone who connects. What is this attack called?');
VALUES ('f76952e0-1bb3-4e07-a6d5-2878a3042661', '513eaf59-8bc0-48bb-8289-c1924e03254e', 'An evil twin attack specifically involves setting up a rogue wireless access point that impersonates a legitimate one — typically broadcasting the same or a very similar SSID, often with a stronger signal specifically to win the race for nearby devices'' auto-connect behavior — so that unsuspecting victims connect to the attacker''s access point instead of the genuine network. Once connected, the attacker sits in the middle of all the victim''s traffic, able to intercept, monitor, or manipulate it (a wireless-specific instance of a broader on-path/man-in-the-middle position). This is a real, technically straightforward attack (multiple access points broadcasting the identical SSID is technically trivial to set up, not physically impossible) and a well-documented risk specifically in public Wi-Fi settings like coffee shops and airports, which is why security guidance commonly recommends using a VPN (covered in Paper 2) on any public Wi-Fi regardless of how legitimate the network appears — the VPN''s encryption protects traffic content even if the underlying network connection itself is compromised or malicious. A worm (self-replicating malware, covered in Paper 1) and a buffer overflow (a memory-safety vulnerability class) are both unrelated, different technical concepts from this network-impersonation attack.');

-- ── Domain: Security Architecture (18% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('96fbb305-8b01-487f-bcfb-e39f1b8088a1', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 6, 'A company runs a highly sensitive industrial control system that must NEVER be reachable from the internet or the corporate business network under any circumstances, even if every firewall rule were somehow misconfigured. What network architecture approach provides this level of guarantee?');
VALUES ('96fbb305-8b01-487f-bcfb-e39f1b8088a1', '44cd721b-6c88-4a21-bf80-c59b78c70ff3', 'An air-gapped network has NO physical network connectivity at all to other networks — this is a fundamentally stronger guarantee than any firewall/rule-based control, because a firewall (no matter how strictly configured) is still a software/logical control that COULD, in principle, be misconfigured, exploited, or bypassed — the network path physically exists even if heavily restricted. An air gap removes the network path entirely, making remote network-based compromise physically impossible, which is exactly the "even if every firewall rule were somehow misconfigured" guarantee the question asks for. This doesn''t make the system invulnerable to EVERY threat — a classic air-gap-defeating vector is a compromised removable media device (like a USB drive) being physically connected to bridge the gap (this is a real, historically significant attack pattern, e.g., Stuxnet) — but it does eliminate the specific NETWORK-based attack surface entirely. A firewall, however well-configured, is not equivalent to a true physical air gap. A VPN creates encrypted CONNECTIVITY between networks — the functional opposite of eliminating connectivity, making it fundamentally the wrong tool for this specific requirement.');

VALUES ('dc07967e-3656-47ee-a126-365f96dc7c39', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 7, 'A company runs multiple untrusted, third-party application workloads on shared infrastructure and wants each workload isolated so that a compromise or bug in one cannot directly access another workload''s memory, files, or processes, while still being more lightweight/efficient than running each in a fully separate virtual machine. What technology provides this middle-ground isolation?');
VALUES ('dc07967e-3656-47ee-a126-365f96dc7c39', 'b81a94ee-e71d-478f-a323-b0447c10d0b0', 'Containers use OS-level isolation features (like Linux namespaces and cgroups) to give each workload its own isolated view of the filesystem, process tree, and (typically) network interfaces, WITHOUT each workload needing its own full, separate operating system kernel the way a traditional VM does — each container shares the underlying host kernel, which is exactly what makes containers more lightweight and faster to start than VMs, at the real trade-off of a (generally) larger shared-kernel attack surface than the stronger isolation boundary a full VM''s separate kernel provides (an important, testable security nuance — containers are NOT considered as strong an isolation boundary as a VM, just a meaningfully STRONGER one than no isolation at all). Running processes with no isolation at all on the shared host is exactly the anti-pattern this question describes wanting to avoid — a bug or compromise in one unisolated process could directly access another''s memory/files with nothing preventing it. A VPN provides encrypted network CONNECTIVITY between separate networks/endpoints — an entirely different function from isolating workloads running on the SAME physical host, a common category-confusion distractor.');

VALUES ('15876a35-0a75-4c22-b996-e793e00c44b6', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 8, 'A company is evaluating whether to use a public cloud provider, build a private cloud in their own data center, or use a hybrid combination of both. From a security responsibility standpoint, how does the division of security responsibility differ between these models?');
VALUES ('15876a35-0a75-4c22-b996-e793e00c44b6', '62e5ef28-7a58-448d-b843-04fc3c109362', 'The shared responsibility model is a foundational public-cloud security concept: the cloud provider is responsible for security "OF the cloud" (physical data centers, underlying hardware, host infrastructure, and for managed services, increasingly more of the stack), while the customer remains responsible for security "IN the cloud" (their own data, correctly configuring access controls/IAM, network settings, and their own application code) — critically, a large share of real-world cloud security incidents stem from CUSTOMER-side misconfiguration (like an overly permissive storage bucket), not a failure on the provider''s side, which is exactly why understanding this DIVISION of responsibility (not assuming the provider handles everything) matters. A private cloud, run entirely within an organization''s own infrastructure with no external provider, means the organization bears responsibility for EVERY layer itself — physical security, hardware, hypervisor, OS, application — with no shared-responsibility offload available, generally MORE total responsibility on the organization, not less (private cloud still has a real attack surface — network-based attacks, insider threats, and physical security risks all still apply). A hybrid model combines both, with the division applying separately to each portion.');

VALUES ('7b2050da-a4e8-4202-9c10-40ea09176b43', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 20, 'A company has several servers in a private subnet that administrators occasionally need to reach for management purposes. Rather than exposing each private server directly to the internet, they deploy a single, hardened, closely-monitored server in a public subnet that administrators connect to first, and from which they then connect onward to the private servers. What is this hardened intermediary server called?');
VALUES ('7b2050da-a4e8-4202-9c10-40ea09176b43', 'd265cdb0-b9de-485a-8972-db5cfc5e316d', 'A bastion host is a deliberately minimal, hardened, and closely monitored server that serves as the SOLE controlled gateway into a private network segment — administrators connect to the bastion first (itself subject to strict access controls, MFA, and logging), then connect onward from there to the actual private target servers, which themselves never need direct internet exposure at all. This dramatically reduces the overall attack surface: instead of every private server individually needing to be hardened against direct internet-facing attacks, security effort concentrates on making the ONE bastion host as resilient and well-monitored as possible, since it is the single path any legitimate (or attempted illegitimate) access must go through. (Note: AWS Systems Manager Session Manager, covered in AWS SAA Paper 4, is a more modern alternative to a traditional bastion host that removes the need for even that one server, but the bastion host CONCEPT itself remains a foundational, frequently-tested architecture pattern.) A honeynet (covered in Paper 2) is a deliberate DECOY environment for attracting and studying attackers — an entirely different purpose from providing legitimate administrative access. A DLP appliance (covered earlier in this paper) inspects content for sensitive data patterns — also unrelated to providing network access pathways.');

-- ── Domain: Security Operations (28% — 6 questions) ──

VALUES ('4e248a5b-2e64-4513-a10d-160f7e3930ca', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 9, 'A company wants to retain security logs from all systems for a full year for potential future investigation, but currently each system stores its own logs locally with wildly inconsistent retention periods, and some systems overwrite their logs after just a few days. What operational practice addresses this?');
VALUES ('4e248a5b-2e64-4513-a10d-160f7e3930ca', 'd7232477-7e9e-48bb-9dc5-11e8e874fb73', 'Centralized log management solves exactly this problem: rather than relying on each individual system''s own local, inconsistent (and often too-short, storage-constrained) default log retention, logs are forwarded off the originating system to a central repository where a SINGLE, deliberately chosen retention policy applies uniformly — this is typically the same log pipeline feeding a SIEM (covered in Paper 1) for real-time correlation, doubling as the long-term retention/archival solution. This also has a real security benefit beyond just retention consistency: centralizing logs off the originating system makes it harder for an attacker who compromises a single system to also delete or tamper with the evidence of their own activity, since the authoritative copy lives elsewhere. Disabling logging entirely is the direct opposite of good security practice — it would eliminate the exact evidence needed for any future investigation, the worst possible response to this scenario. Log retention absolutely CAN be centrally configured — this is a real, standard, widely implemented practice specifically because per-system default settings are so often inconsistent or too short.');

VALUES ('334bd578-6b7f-43d7-a2ef-74909580d97d', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 10, 'A vulnerability scan identifies 200 findings across a company''s systems in a single scan. The security team cannot realistically fix all 200 immediately. What structured process determines which vulnerabilities to remediate first, and what factors should drive that prioritization?');
VALUES ('334bd578-6b7f-43d7-a2ef-74909580d97d', '5fa880ae-f6a7-4cf6-8afe-0188622c750b', 'The vulnerability management lifecycle extends beyond just scanning (identification, covered as vulnerability SCANNING specifically in Paper 1) into a full structured process: after identifying findings, they must be PRIORITIZED — genuinely useful prioritization weighs severity (often via CVSS score), REAL-WORLD exploitability (a vulnerability with a known, actively-used exploit deserves more urgency than a theoretical one with none), and the specific asset''s exposure/criticality (an internet-facing critical system needs faster attention than an isolated internal one with a technically identical vulnerability) — not simply working through findings in whatever arbitrary order a scanning tool happens to list them. After remediation, VERIFICATION (re-scanning or otherwise confirming the fix actually took effect) closes the loop — a patch marked "applied" that didn''t actually apply successfully is a real, common failure mode this step exists to catch. Not every finding from a scan carries equal real-world risk — treating a low-severity, internal-only finding identically to a critical, internet-facing one wastes limited remediation capacity on the wrong priorities first.');

VALUES ('9e7802fd-c5e2-4731-9905-179a507f0df2', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 11, 'A security team subscribes to external feeds listing known-malicious IP addresses, file hashes of known malware, and domains associated with active phishing campaigns, and automatically feeds this data into their SIEM to flag matches against their own network traffic and logs. What is this category of information called?');
VALUES ('9e7802fd-c5e2-4731-9905-179a507f0df2', '7909f8f8-e3f3-4119-8c30-f4e36cc20dde', 'Threat intelligence refers broadly to externally-sourced knowledge about current threats, and Indicators of Compromise (IOCs) are the specific, concrete, machine-matchable artifacts within that intelligence — known-malicious IP addresses, malware file hashes, phishing domains, suspicious URL patterns — that can be automatically compared against an organization''s own logs/traffic to detect activity associated with a KNOWN threat, often much faster than that organization independently discovering and analyzing the same threat from first principles. Feeding IOCs into a SIEM (covered in Paper 1) for automatic matching is a standard, real, and valuable practice — despite IOCs having a real, finite useful lifespan as attackers rotate infrastructure (which is exactly why feeds are CONTINUOUSLY updated, not one-time static lists), they still provide genuine, actionable value during their valid window. Digital forensics is post-incident evidence investigation (covered in Paper 1) — a different activity from consuming external threat feeds. A BIA (covered in Paper 2) analyzes business-function criticality/impact, entirely unrelated to IOC feeds.');

VALUES ('bb3c9863-825c-4300-899c-ab9ec6ed6f5f', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 12, 'A database administrator needs elevated (admin-level) access to a production database only occasionally, for specific maintenance tasks, rather than holding standing admin privileges at all times. The company wants that elevated access to be requested, time-limited, and automatically revoked when the task is done or the time window expires. What security practice/technology category addresses this?');
VALUES ('bb3c9863-825c-4300-899c-ab9ec6ed6f5f', '037f8fc1-4319-46b1-8767-690e82b1253b', 'PAM solutions are purpose-built for exactly this "just-in-time" privileged access pattern: instead of an account holding standing, always-available elevated privileges (a real, persistent risk — if that account is ever compromised, the attacker immediately has full elevated access with no additional step needed), PAM requires a specific REQUEST for elevation, often with an approval step, grants the elevated privilege only for a defined, limited time window, and automatically revokes it afterward — directly shrinking the window during which elevated access could be misused, whether by an external attacker who compromised the account or by insider risk. This is a real, increasingly standard practice specifically because standing privileged access is a disproportionately attractive target and a common root cause in real breaches once an attacker gains any foothold on a permanently-privileged account. A VPN provides encrypted network connectivity (covered in Paper 2) — entirely unrelated to privilege elevation/revocation timing. Standing, permanent admin access is exactly the anti-pattern PAM exists to reduce, not a recommended practice.');

VALUES ('cda98e8d-1932-4035-8a2c-eebb21bc5baa', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 13, 'A company wants to automatically detect and block an employee from emailing a spreadsheet containing customer credit card numbers to a personal external email address, whether accidental or deliberate. What security technology category is designed for this?');
VALUES ('cda98e8d-1932-4035-8a2c-eebb21bc5baa', '345d6e3c-d2d5-4e35-9f29-c384e4f18707', 'DLP is specifically designed to inspect the actual CONTENT of data (not just its network metadata) — scanning for patterns matching sensitive data types (credit card number formats, SSN patterns, specific document classification labels covered in Paper 2''s data classification content) across data in motion (email, file uploads, web traffic), data at rest (scanning file shares/storage for sensitive data sitting where it shouldn''t be), and data in use (blocking a copy/paste or USB transfer of sensitive content) — and can take automated action (block, quarantine, require justification, alert security) BEFORE the sensitive data actually leaves organizational control, addressing both accidental (a well-meaning employee''s mistake) and deliberate exfiltration in the same control. A standard firewall filters traffic by IP/port/protocol (covered in Paper 1) and has no content-aware inspection of email body/attachment CONTENT for sensitive data patterns by default — a different, network-layer function from DLP''s content-layer inspection. This is a real, purpose-built, and commonly deployed security control category specifically because this exact scenario (accidental or deliberate sensitive-data email leakage) is a common real-world incident type.');

VALUES ('3837b3b1-6fb6-41c7-b492-a6d12b9becbb', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 14, 'A company''s data center has a battery-backed UPS (Uninterruptible Power Supply) to bridge any momentary power loss, plus a diesel generator that automatically starts if power remains out for more than a few seconds, ensuring critical systems keep running through a real, extended power outage. What broader security/operations concept does this physical infrastructure redundancy support?');
VALUES ('3837b3b1-6fb6-41c7-b492-a6d12b9becbb', 'b9408113-5b31-410e-9c5b-df19f4ed18be', 'This is a direct, concrete, physical-layer example of supporting Availability — one-third of the CIA triad (covered in Paper 1) — since a system that goes down during a power outage is, by definition, not available to its authorized users, regardless of how well confidentiality or integrity are otherwise protected. UPS and generator redundancy specifically address the physical dependency (electricity) that every other digital control ultimately relies on — no amount of network security or encryption matters if the servers have no power at all. This directly supports the broader goal of Continuity of Operations (keeping critical functions running through a disruption, connecting to BCP concepts covered in Paper 1), and is a genuinely important reminder that security is not purely a digital/network-layer discipline — physical infrastructure resilience is a real, foundational, and frequently underappreciated part of the same availability goal. It has no relationship to confidentiality (secrecy of data) or non-repudiation (proving who did what) — those are entirely different CIA-triad/AAA-framework concepts (covered earlier in this paper) from availability.');

-- ── Domain: Security Program Management & Oversight (20% — 4 questions) ──

VALUES ('25a4a6ec-89d0-462c-b66d-fafc60fb12c1', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 15, 'A company outsources payroll processing to a third-party vendor who will have access to employee Social Security numbers and bank account details. Before signing the contract, what should the company do to understand and manage the security risk this vendor relationship introduces?');
VALUES ('25a4a6ec-89d0-462c-b66d-fafc60fb12c1', '5313c5b1-78ba-475e-ade2-cc1063195077', 'Third-party/vendor risk management recognizes a genuinely important reality: once sensitive data is shared with a vendor, that vendor''s OWN security posture directly becomes a risk to the originating company''s data — a company can have excellent internal security and still suffer a real breach because a vendor they shared data with was compromised (a well-documented real-world pattern in actual breach history). Proper due diligence BEFORE granting access — reviewing the vendor''s compliance certifications/third-party audit reports (covered in Paper 2), understanding their own security practices, and establishing clear contractual security requirements and ongoing monitoring — is the standard proactive practice specifically to manage this transferred risk before it becomes a problem, not after. This is why "our vendor got breached" is not treated as fully absolving the originating company of responsibility in most regulatory/contractual frameworks — the originating company chose that vendor and is expected to have assessed the risk. Waiting until after a vendor breach to assess risk defeats the entire preventive purpose of vendor risk management.');

VALUES ('daa8e1b8-dc8c-4818-9465-55a5a54a2f21', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 16, 'A system administrator makes an unreviewed, unapproved firewall rule change directly on a production system to "quickly fix" a connectivity issue, inadvertently opening a port that should have remained closed. What governance process, if followed, would have caught this before it introduced risk?');
VALUES ('daa8e1b8-dc8c-4818-9465-55a5a54a2f21', '4efcb516-5b07-47f9-bfa7-ee491fce5c18', 'Change management is the formal governance process specifically designed to catch exactly this kind of problem BEFORE it reaches production: a proposed change (a firewall rule, a configuration update, a code deployment) is documented, reviewed by someone other than the person making the change (often via a Change Advisory Board or structured peer review), and formally approved — the review step is precisely what catches unintended consequences like an inadvertently-opened port that the original administrator, focused narrowly on "quickly fixing" the immediate connectivity issue, didn''t notice or consider. This applies broadly to ANY production change with risk implications — infrastructure configuration (firewall rules, network settings) just as much as application code deployments, not exclusively one or the other. Skipping change management entirely in the name of speed/convenience is exactly the anti-pattern that leads to incidents like this one — the process exists specifically because unreviewed, ad-hoc production changes are a real, common, and preventable source of security and operational incidents, not a theoretical concern.');

VALUES ('3f1df87c-fc89-449e-a227-04d473998896', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 17, 'A company is notified of active pending litigation that may require producing certain emails and documents as evidence. Despite the company''s normal data retention policy calling for deletion of emails after 90 days, they must now preserve all potentially relevant data indefinitely until the matter is resolved. What is this requirement called?');
VALUES ('3f1df87c-fc89-449e-a227-04d473998896', '011873cc-6a2d-42ce-ac70-aa906879d1a3', 'A legal hold is a real, legally significant obligation that suspends an organization''s NORMAL data retention/destruction schedule for any data potentially relevant to pending or reasonably anticipated litigation, regulatory investigation, or audit — continuing to delete data on a normal schedule AFTER a legal hold obligation has been triggered can constitute spoliation of evidence, carrying real legal consequences (adverse inference, sanctions) independent of the underlying litigation''s outcome. This is exactly why a legal hold OVERRIDES the routine 90-day policy described in this scenario for the specifically affected data — normal retention policy and legal hold obligations are related concepts (both about how long data is kept) but a legal hold is a distinct, externally-triggered, legally-mandated exception, not merely a variant of the routine policy. A BIA (covered in Paper 2) analyzes business-function criticality/impact — an entirely unrelated concept with no bearing on litigation-driven data preservation obligations.');

VALUES ('a1f74a98-fb86-4e42-b762-e8fe49d0e67d', '64cd3780-27fe-4f0d-8f74-c33028cf77eb', 18, 'A company requires that the employee who initiates a wire transfer request can never be the SAME employee who approves it — a second, different person must always independently review and approve before funds move. What security principle does this represent, and how does it differ from least privilege?');
VALUES ('a1f74a98-fb86-4e42-b762-e8fe49d0e67d', 'ad92e890-9a0b-47a1-9084-804a08613e3a', 'Separation of duties (sometimes called segregation of duties) specifically requires that a sensitive, high-risk process be split across multiple people, such that no single individual holds enough combined authority/access to complete the entire sensitive action alone — directly reducing both the risk of deliberate fraud (a single corrupt actor cannot both initiate AND approve a fraudulent transfer alone) and the impact of an honest mistake (a second reviewer has a genuine opportunity to catch an error before it takes effect). This is a genuinely distinct concept from least privilege (covered in Paper 1): least privilege limits how MUCH access any one person has, but even a correctly-scoped, appropriately-privileged single individual could still complete an entire sensitive process alone if separation of duties isn''t ALSO enforced — the two principles work together but address different risks (excess access vs. single-point-of-failure trust in one person). Non-repudiation (covered earlier in this paper, via digital signatures) is about proving who took an action after the fact, not about requiring multiple people to jointly authorize an action in the first place — a different concept entirely.');
